      "tips": [
        {
          "text": "Make sure to have a refined resume, a good cgpa (I’d recommend having 8+, but try to keep at least 7.5+ otherwise you’ll miss out on a few), some achievements to get shortlisted.",
          "category": "Interview strategy",
          "page": 1
        },
        {
          "text": "Along with a good resume also try to keep linkedin and github maintained.",
          "category": "Interview strategy",
          "page": 1
        },
        {
          "text": "Regular aptitude practice (IndiaBix, common OA Questions) and brushing up on core CS topics (DBMS, OS basics, OOP) is sufficient.",
          "category": "OA preparation",
          "page": 3
        },
        {
          "text": "For the coding questions, having a command of stacks, arrays, binary search and basic bit tricks helps, you can extrapolate to covering linear structures and basic sorting algorithms.",
          "category": "Coding preparation",
          "page": 3
        },
        {
          "text": "Be ready to explain the what, how, and why for each project — not just what you implemented but design tradeoffs and alternate approaches.",
          "category": "Project preparation",
          "page": 4
        },
        {
          "text": "Do not pretend to know an optimized solution even if you do — state the brute-force first, then show how you would improve it.",
          "category": "Coding preparation",
          "page": 5
        },
        {
          "text": "DO NOT SAY ANY THINGS THAT YOU ARE NOT AWARE OF. It is always good to say that you don’t know rather than saying something wrong or the interviewer sensing that you are not much aware of the topic.",
          "category": "Interview strategy",
          "page": 5
        },
        {
          "text": "Think out loud, ask for constraints, write the brute force approach and then optimize, and write pseudo-code once the approach is agreed upon. Test your solution with 1 example.",
          "category": "Coding preparation",
          "page": 6
        },
        {
          "text": "Be crisp about your projects. When you mention an implementation detail casually, the panel will latch on to it and probe deeper — so be deliberate with your words.",
          "category": "Project preparation",
          "page": 6
        },
        {
          "text": "Explain tradeoffs instead of one-line answers.",
          "category": "Technical preparation",
          "page": 6
        }
      ],
